# llm-gateway.env.example — copy to llm-gateway.env and fill in.
#
# Implements: SR-043, LLR-963, LLR-964
#
# THE REAL FILE IS GITIGNORED AND MUST STAY THAT WAY. It holds every provider
# credential the household has plus the key that decrypts them at rest, which is
# the single densest concentration of secrets anywhere in this stack. Nothing in
# here is a placeholder you may leave as-is.
#
# It is materialised by the deploy tooling like every other secret-bearing file;
# do not hand-edit it on the box and expect that to survive a redeploy.

# ── WHAT IS *NOT* SET HERE, AND WHY ──────────────────────────────────────────
# An earlier version of this file declared LLM_GATEWAY_API_KEY,
# LLM_GATEWAY_ADMIN_EMAIL and LLM_GATEWAY_ADMIN_PASSWORD. Upstream reads none of
# those names. They were our names, and the test that "checked" them only read
# this same file back — it asserted our invention against our invention and
# could never have failed. The names below were taken from upstream's own
# .env.example and from the pinned image's config.
#
# THE BEARER KEY IS NOT AN ENVIRONMENT VARIABLE. Upstream's unified API key is
# minted in the dashboard and stored in its database; there is no env var that
# sets it. So it cannot be pre-seeded from here, and the Caddy site's decision
# to add no basic_auth still holds — but the key it defers to only exists once
# somebody has created it in the dashboard. Mint it there at first setup,
# BEFORE pointing any consumer at the gateway.
#
# THE ADMIN ACCOUNT IS NOT AN ENVIRONMENT VARIABLE EITHER. The first account is
# created through the dashboard. Because this install is reachable from other
# devices, upstream also requires a ONE-TIME SETUP CODE, printed in the server
# log at startup while no account exists:
#
#     docker logs llm-gateway 2>&1 | grep -i setup
#
# That code is what stops a stranger claiming a freshly exposed install, and it
# is the reason first setup is a hands-on step rather than a minted secret.

# ── at-rest encryption ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ENCRYPTION_KEY — upstream's name, not a prefixed one of ours.
#
# MINT THIS BEFORE THE FIRST START. Older notes in this repo said to let the
# container run once and see whether it generated one; that advice came from
# upstream's non-production path and does not apply here. The pinned image sets
# NODE_ENV=production in its own config, and upstream documents this key as
# REQUIRED in production. Unset, the ordering worry it described cannot arise.
#
# 64 hex characters (32 bytes):
#     openssl rand -hex 32
#
# Provider keys are stored AES-256-GCM-encrypted in the SQLite database and
# decrypted in memory per request. IF YOU LOSE THIS YOU LOSE THE STORED PROVIDER
# KEYS — the database is then undecryptable and every provider must be
# re-entered. Keep a copy off the hub: it lives in the volume's backup, which is
# the local set and never the offsite one, and a key whose only copy sits beside
# the data it decrypts is not a backup of anything.
ENCRYPTION_KEY=

# ── provider credentials ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Add one per provider you enable, using the names upstream's Keys page expects.
# LEAVE UNUSED PROVIDERS ABSENT RATHER THAN BLANK: an empty value can read as a
# configured-but-broken provider and produce failures that look like rate limits.
#
# Free tiers may carry anything (Owner, 2026-09-17) — there is no local-only
# routing constraint, which is what makes the fallback in SR-044's held-request
# path always available.
#
# Examples, commented out:
# GROQ_API_KEY=
# CEREBRAS_API_KEY=
# MISTRAL_API_KEY=
# OPENROUTER_API_KEY=
# GOOGLE_API_KEY=

# ── the local model on the dev PC ────────────────────────────────────────────
# Added through the gateway's Keys page as a custom OpenAI-compatible endpoint
# (IF-021), not here, because upstream stores backend definitions in its own
# database. Recorded in this file only as a reminder that the endpoint is
# http://<dev-pc>:<inference-port>/v1 and that the dev PC's server must run as a
# SERVICE — after a cold wake there is no user session for it to live in.
